{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"When a U.S. ’40 Act ETF liquidates, shareholders generally receive their pro-rata share of the fund’s **net assets**—after liabilities and liquidation costs—not automatically their original investment or the latest quoted share price. You can lose money if the assets’ value has fallen; liquidation itself does not ordinarily mean forfeiting the whole holding.\n\nTrading may stop before the fund distributes cash. The ETF typically sells or otherwise disposes of its portfolio, then pays shareholders based on shares held on the applicable record date. The distribution often follows within days or weeks after trading ends, but timing varies; check the fund’s liquidation notice and your broker.\n\nIn a taxable account, the cash distribution generally triggers a taxable gain or loss measured against your adjusted cost basis, and any taxable gains realized inside the fund may also be passed through. Tax treatment depends on the distribution and your circumstances; consult a tax professional."}}
